[38;2;124;124;124mAnd $F_\varepsilon \xrightarrow{\Gamma} F$ (Gamma-convergence).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mQuestion: Is $F$ coercive?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mCoercive means: for all $t \in \mathbb{R}$, the sublevel set $\{x : F(x) \le [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mt\}$ is (contained in a) compact set, or more precisely, $\{x : F(x) \le t\}$ is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcompact (or precompact). Actually, the standard definition: $F$ is coercive if [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfor every $t$, $\{x : F(x) \le t\}$ is contained in a compact set (or is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact). Let me use the same definition as given: $\{x : F(x) \le t\} \subset [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mK_t$ for some compact $K_t$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me prove that $F$ is coercive.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTake any $t \in \mathbb{R}$. We want to show $\{x : F(x) \le t\}$ is contained [0m
[38;2;124;124;124min a compact set.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy equicoercivity, there exists compact $K_t$ such that for all $\varepsilon$, $[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\{x : F_\varepsilon(x) \le t\} \subset K_t$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mClaim: $\{x : F(x) \le t\} \subset K_t$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSuppose $F(u) \le t$. By the recovery sequence condition (condition 2 of Gamma-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconvergence), there exists a sequence $\{u_\varepsilon\}$ converging to $u$ such[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthat $F(u) = \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} F_\varepsilon(u_\varepsilon)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} F_\varepsilon(u_\varepsilon) = F(u) \le t$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis means for sufficiently small $\varepsilon$ (say $\varepsilon < [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\varepsilon_0$), $F_\varepsilon(u_\varepsilon) \le t + \delta$ for some small $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\delta > 0$, or more precisely, since the limit is $\le t$, for any $\delta > [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m0$, for small enough $\varepsilon$, $F_\varepsilon(u_\varepsilon) \le t + [0m
